   "endingProse": "*The three stand frozen in the rain. Masago grips the bandit's sword, her knuckles white, demanding her husband speak his shame; Takehiro strains against his bonds, reaching for the weapon that offers him a warrior's death; Tajomaru steps back, having successfully planted the seed of discord by pointing out the true source of Masago's agony—her husband's gaze. No blade has struck a final blow yet, but the silence betw*",
   "fullProse": "# In a Grove\n\n## Background\n\nThe bandit Tajomaru lured the samurai Takehiro and his wife Masago into a secluded grove, bound Takehiro to a cedar, and assaulted Masago before his eyes. The act is finished, but the violence has not ceased. Tajomaru’s longsword stands in the mud, Masago conceals a small dagger, and Takehiro remains bound, his silence louder than any scream. The three are frozen in a stalemate of shame and rage, waiting to see who will move to kill or to die.\n\n## Characters\n- **Tajomaru** (bandit): A notorious bandit of the mountain roads, known for both prowess and cunning. — wants: Walk away whole, without looking base — better to end this as a 'duel' than a slaughter.\n- **Masago** (the samurai's wife): Takehiro's wife, robbed while traveling at her husband's side. — wants: To never again be looked at like that; to bring this shame to some end.\n- **Takehiro** (samurai): A samurai traveling with his wife, caught in the trap and bound. — wants: To claw back a shred of a warrior's dignity from this humiliation — even at the price of his own death.\n\n## SCENE 1\n*A shaded bamboo grove. Midday light filters through the leaves, illuminating the mud. Tajomaru's longsword is planted upright in the earth. Masago stands disheveled, her robes torn. Takehiro is bound to a cedar behind her, his head bowed.*\n\n*Masago steps out from the shadow of the cedar, lifting her arms slightly to display her torn kimono. She forces both men to witness the disarray of her dress.*\n\n**Masago**: Do not look away. You both saw it. You stood there and let it happen. There is no erasing this from the air.\n\n**Takehiro**: Takehiro lifts his head slowly, his eyes locking onto Masago with a cold, unblinking stare filled with loathing.\n\n**Takehiro**: I see you. I see all of you. And I see that there is nothing left here worth calling a wife.\n\n**Tajomaru**: Your hand shakes, woman. But not for the sword in the dirt, is it? I see the shape of something else hidden in your sleeve. Something sharp enough to finish this mess one way or the other.\n\n*Masago snatches her hand back, clutching the fabric of her robe tightly over her breast. Takehiro jerks his head up at Tajomaru’s words, staring at the desperate knot of Masago’s fingers.*\n\n## SCENE 2\n*The same grove moments later. The rain begins to fall, drumming against the bamboo leaves. Tajomaru lounges against a tree, watching the exit path. Masago clutches a small dagger in her hand, hidden partly by her sleeve.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: That little blade in your hand decides who dies here, woman. But the horse and goods I have tied beyond the trees... they decide who lives to see the sunset.\n\n*Tajomaru jerks his chin toward the forest path with a smirk. Masago’s eyes dart toward the exit, then back to the bound samurai.*\n\n**Masago**: You think you can just walk away? You think there is a path left for you after this?\n\n*Takehiro ignores them, straining against the ropes. With a sudden, violent twitch, he spits a mouthful of blood onto the earth between them, the red glob splattering near Masago’s hem.*\n\n**Masago**: Do not turn away, bandit. Look at him. Look at the eyes that watched me suffer. There is no pity in them. Only... filth. He looks at his wife and sees only a thing to be discarded. That gaze cuts deeper than any steel you could ever wield.\n\n## SCENE 3\n*The rain slackens, dripping heavily from the broad leaves. Masago crouches in the mud and wrenches Tajomaru's longsword from the earth with a wet sucking sound. She rises, leveling the heavy blade between the two men. Takehiro is bound to the cedar, his chest thrust forward.*\n\n**Masago**: You both look at me with such hunger. One for flesh, the other for judgment. Here—this is the only honest thing between us. Will you duel for it, or will you bleed like cowards while I hold the edge?\n\n**Takehiro**: Takehiro leans his torso forward, offering his heart to the trembling point of the sword.\n\n**Takehiro**: Do not brandish that thing at the air, woman. If you cannot bear the silence in my eyes, then use the bandit's steel. Cut me down. But know that if you strike, you are no victim—you are just another killer in the mud.\n\n*Masago’s knuckles are white around the hilt. She does not raise it to strike, but turns the blade slowly, letting the rain wash the mud from the scabbard. Her free hand hovers over the fold of her robe, where the outline of the thin dagger presses against the silk.*\n\n**Masago**: Honor? There is no honor here, only three bodies and the question of which one falls first. If I cut him, you claim a duel. If I cut myself, you claim a coward's despair. But if the knife moves... and the hand that holds it is lost in the rain... who will be able to say which way the blade went?\n\n## SCENE 4\n*The grove is stark and still, the midday light filtering down to the mud-slicked ground. The coarse rope bites into Takehiro's wrists. Tajomaru's longsword stands upright in the earth. Masago grips the hilt of the longsword with one hand, her other hovering over the hidden dagger at her chest.*\n\n*Masago does not pull the sword free, only holds it, her knuckles white, forcing the weight of the steel to settle between them.*\n\n**Masago**: You look at me as if I were the dirt on your sandals. Speak it, husband. Say that I am the one who is filthy.\n\n**Takehiro**: Takehiro strains against the cedar, ignoring the pain. He twists his body, reaching his bound hands out toward the hilt of Tajomaru's sword, his fingers grasping at the air inches from the steel.\n\n**Takehiro**: If it is written, then let us finish the inscription. My hands are tied, but my eyes are open. I saw everything. I am not a witness to be silenced, nor a body to be left in the grass. Give me the steel, or cut me down where I stand—but do not think we can go back to before.\n\n*Tajomaru steps back, gesturing broadly toward the bound samurai with a sneer, framing Takehiro’s rigid expression for Masago to see clearly.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: I have done the deed, but he is the one cutting you to pieces. Look at him. There is no pity in that face, only disgust. He hates you more for existing than he hates me for what I did.\n\n## Epilogue\n\n*The three stand frozen in the rain. Masago grips the bandit's sword, her knuckles white, demanding her husband speak his shame; Takehiro strains against his bonds, reaching for the weapon that offers him a warrior's death; Tajomaru steps back, having successfully planted the seed of discord by pointing out the true source of Masago's agony—her husband's gaze. No blade has struck a final blow yet, but the silence betw*",
