   "endingProse": "*The samurai stands free but bound by his own contempt, his wife holding a blade to her throat to escape his judgment, and the bandit watching from the shadows. No one has moved to kill, yet the damage is absolute; the bond between husband and wife is severed by a look, and the grove holds them in a silence more suffocating than the rope.*",
   "fullProse": "# In a Grove\n\n## Background\n\nThe notorious bandit Tajomaru lured the samurai Takehiro and his wife Masago into a grove, bound the husband to a cedar, and assaulted Masago before his eyes. The act is finished, but the violence is not. Tajomaru’s longsword stands in the mud, Masago hides a dagger in her sleeve, and Takehiro is tied to the tree, humiliated and impotent. They are frozen in the aftermath, each desperate to salvage a shred of dignity from the ruin, yet unable to leave the grove without settling the debt of what has been done.\n\n## Characters\n- **Tajomaru** (bandit): A notorious bandit of the mountain roads, known for both prowess and cunning. — wants: Walk away whole, without looking base — better to end this as a 'duel' than a slaughter.\n- **Masago** (the samurai's wife): Takehiro's wife, robbed while traveling at her husband's side. — wants: To never again be looked at like that; to bring this shame to some end.\n- **Takehiro** (samurai): A samurai traveling with his wife, caught in the trap and bound. — wants: To claw back a shred of a warrior's dignity from this humiliation — even at the price of his own death.\n\n## SCENE 1\n*Deep in a bamboo grove on a shaded slope. Midday light falls through the leaves. The bandit stands loose and grinning; the samurai is bound to a cedar; the wife sits crumpled in the dirt. Tajomaru's longsword stands upright in the mud between them.*\n\n**Takehiro**: Pick it up, bandit. If you are a warrior, finish this as men do.\n\n**Tajomaru**: Hah! You speak of men’s ways while you trussed like a chicken? The blade stays in the mud until I say otherwise.\n\n*Masago draws the short blade halfway from its sheath, the metal rasping loudly in the grove, and presses the point against her breastbone, eyes locked on Takehiro.*\n\n**Masago**: You look at me as if I were trash left in the gutter. If I am so soiled, then I am not fit for this world—or for yours.\n\n*Tajomaru lunges forward, clamping a hand around Masago's wrist to wrench the dagger from her grasp, the blade flashing between them.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: I’ll not have a corpse on my conscience today! If blood is to be spilled, it will be spilled by my hand, not yours in cowardice.\n\n## SCENE 2\n*The same grove. Masago has twisted free, standing between the men with her hair wild. Takehiro strains against the cedar. Tajomaru holds the dagger now, his longsword thrust point-down into the mud nearby.*\n\n**Masago**: Do not turn your gaze away! You saw it all—you stood there and let it happen. Your silence is louder than any scream. Look at me now and tell me you are still clean!\n\n*Takehiro ignores the stinging words; his body convulses as he strains against the coarse hemp, his fingers scraping the mud to grasp the hilt of the dagger near his feet.*\n\n**Takehiro**: Enough! I will not be lectured on honor by the one who has discarded it. If the world is broken, then I shall be the one to sweep it away.\n\n**Masago**: Do not turn from it, husband. Look at me. Not with pity—pity is for a stranger. Look at me with what you truly feel. It is not sorrow for my pain. It is disgust.\n\n*Tajomaru sneers and kicks the longsword through the mud until it clatters against Takehiro’s knees. He looms over the samurai, pressing the tip of the small dagger into the soft hollow of Takehiro's throat.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: You hear her, Samurai? She sees the truth. You sit there tied like a calf, yet you look down on her? Pick up your sword. Kill me, or kill her, but stop looking at her like she is dung while you do nothing.\n\n## SCENE 3\n*The grove. The rain drums harder against the canopy. Masago stands between the men, her robes heavy with water and the weight of the stolen longsword hidden beneath them.*\n\n*Masago’s hand darts out, snatching the longsword from the mud. She sweeps it backward, concealing the long blade within the folds of her wet robes.*\n\n**Masago**: You both speak of honor as if it were a stone one could hold in the hand. But I see only steel waiting to drink. If this blade is to decide a man's worth, let it not be done while he is trussed like a chicken for the pot.\n\n**Tajomaru**: Look at her, Samurai. Your wife has stolen your fangs. She hides the steel that could wash away your shame—or cut your throat while you beg. Do you still call her innocent?\n\n**Masago**: Do not pretend you are trapped here with us. Your beast waits by the road, laden with our goods, ready to carry a thief away clean while we rot in the mud.\n\n**Takehiro**: Let him ride. Let him take the silver and the silk. If I leave this grove on that beast, I leave as a man who bought his life with his wife's shame. I would rather the crows have me.\n\n## SCENE 4\n*The grove. Rain drums against the cedar leaves. Takehiro is bound to the tree; Masago stands apart, the dagger hidden in her sleeve. Tajomaru paces, restless.*\n\n**Takehiro**: You clutch that steel as if it could wash you clean. But the rain cannot wash away what we all saw here. No blade can carve that memory from your skin—or mine.\n\n*Tajomaru strides to the cedar, grabs the knot binding Takehiro, and yanks it tight for a moment before deliberately loosening it with his knife. The rope snaps and falls into the mud.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: The knot is bitten through. If you are still a man, Takehiro, stand up and finish this.\n\n*Takehiro stands straight, rubbing his raw wrists, and locks eyes with Masago. He does not reach for a weapon.*\n\n**Takehiro**: You saw it. We all stood here and let the sun go down on it. There is no going back to before.\n\n## SCENE 5\n*The grove. The rain seems to hush. Takehiro stands free, Tajomaru leans against the cedar, and Masago faces them, trembling with rage and despair.*\n\n*Takehiro turns his gaze squarely upon Masago, deliberately letting his expression shift from shock to a cold, unblinking stare of pure disgust.*\n\n**Masago**: Masago flinches as if struck. She does not retreat; instead, she pulls the dagger from her sleeve and presses the point hard against the skin of her own throat, stepping closer to Takehiro until she is breathing in his face.\n\n*Tajomaru leans back against the cedar, watching the standoff with a dark, unreadable fascination, his hand hovering loosely over his own hilt.*\n\n## Epilogue\n\n*The samurai stands free but bound by his own contempt, his wife holding a blade to her throat to escape his judgment, and the bandit watching from the shadows. No one has moved to kill, yet the damage is absolute; the bond between husband and wife is severed by a look, and the grove holds them in a silence more suffocating than the rope.*",
