   "fullProse": "# In a Grove\n\n## Background\n\nIn the shade of a bamboo grove, the bandit Tajomaru has lured a samurai, Takehiro, and his wife, Masago, into a trap. Takehiro is bound to a cedar tree, forced to watch while Tajomaru assaults Masago. The deed is done. The rain falls, turning the ground to mud. Tajomaru has retrieved his longsword, Masago clutches a small dagger, and Takehiro remains tied, his dignity in tatters. The three are frozen in a triangle of shame and rage, waiting to see who will make the next move.\n\n## Characters\n- **Tajomaru** (bandit): A notorious bandit of the mountain roads, known for both prowess and cunning. — wants: Walk away whole, without looking base — better to end this as a 'duel' than a slaughter.\n- **Masago** (the samurai's wife): Takehiro's wife, robbed while traveling at her husband's side. — wants: To never again be looked at like that; to bring this shame to some end.\n- **Takehiro** (samurai): A samurai traveling with his wife, caught in the trap and bound. — wants: To claw back a shred of a warrior's dignity from this humiliation — even at the price of his own death.\n\n## SCENE 1\n*Deep in a bamboo grove. Midday light struggles through the thick bamboo canopy. The ground is slick with mud. TAKEHIRO is bound to a large cedar. TAJOMARU stands loose in the clearing, his longsword in hand. MASAGO crouches nearby, a small dagger gripped in her trembling hand.*\n\n*Masago pulls the small dagger from her robe with a trembling but decisive hand. She presses the cold steel against the pulse in her neck, her eyes fixed on the space between the two men.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: Do not stain my victory with a widow's wail. Look at him, woman! He has hands, he has a sword—let him fight for your honor if he is man enough, or die like the dog he has become.\n\n**Takehiro**: You speak of stories? Look at this edge, you dog. It is thirsty. If it drinks today, no tongue left alive will be able to say whose hand guided it—yours, mine, or hers.\n\n*Masago wrenches her arm violently from Takehiro's grasp, stumbling back before sprinting past Tajomaru, her eyes fixed on the gap in the trees where the path leads out.*\n\n**Masago**: I will take your horse! I will leave you both here to rot in your honor!\n\n*Tajomaru lunges from his stance, boots sliding in the mud as he dashes across the clearing. He throws his arms wide, planting his body squarely between Masago and the exit.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: You touch nothing of mine! The beast stays, and so do you, until the accounts are settled!\n\n## SCENE 2\n*The same place. The rain drums against the bamboo, masking the heavy breathing of the three figures. The samurai is bound to the cedar; the bandit stands loose; the wife crouches near the mud, her hand hovering over the hilt of the stolen sword.*\n\n**Masago**: It ends here. Not in blood, and not for the pride of thieves. I will not have this ground soaked again because you two need to prove you are men.\n\n*Masago’s fingers clamp around the longsword’s hilt, dragging it through the wet earth to tuck it beneath the hem of her robe, hiding the steel from the men’s eyes.*\n\n*Tajomaru lunges, his hand closing around Masago’s wrist with a grip of iron, wrenching the weapon free and ripping it from her grasp. He holds the scabbard high, the rain pattering against the lacquered wood.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: Put it away? A woman does not sheathe a warrior’s fate. This blade decides if I am a rat or a lord, and I will not let you bury it in the dirt to save your shame.\n\n**Takehiro**: You speak of rats and lords, yet you hold the steel while I stand bound. If that blade is the measure of a man, give it to me. Let us see if your courage matches your tongue, or if you are just a dog barking at a tied wolf.\n\n*Masago whips the dagger from her sleeve and presses the cold steel against the skin of her throat, leaning forward so Takehiro cannot look away.*\n\n**Masago**: You cannot bear to see me, can you? Your eyes do not look at a wife, but at a stain you wish washed away. Very well. If I am filth, I will scrub myself from your sight forever.\n\n**Tajomaru**: Look at him, woman. He does not tremble for your life, he only turns his face from the shame of it. That is not a husband’s grief; that is a master judging a broken vase.\n\n## SCENE 3\n*The rain falls in erratic gusts, deafening the grove. The ground is a quagmire. Masago is covered in mud, the dagger in her hand. Tajomaru blocks the path to the trees, his posture aggressive. Takehiro strains against his bonds, the rope creaking audibly.*\n\n*Tajomaru stamps his boots heavily into the mud, planting himself like a wall between Masago and the forest path. He guards the exit with his life.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: That horse is my feet! That beast carries my silver, my gear, my clean way out! You think you can ride my stolen property to wash your hands? You will stay here in the mud with me!\n\n*Masago screams, a raw sound that tears through the rain, and lunges forward with the dagger leveled at Tajomaru’s chest. She drives him back with the sheer weight of her desperation, scrambling past him toward the tethered animal.*\n\n**Masago**: You think you can just walk away? Look at me! Look at what you did here! You cannot wash that off with rain or ride away from it on stolen legs!\n\n*Tajomaru snarls and drops his shoulder, slamming into Masago with brutal force to knock her away from the exit. He stomps his foot down on the muddy turf, asserting dominance over the only way out.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: It is the only clean thing left in this stinking grove, and I decide who touches it!\n\n*Takehiro straightens his spine against the rough bark of the cedar, twisting his wrists against the loosening rope to stand as tall as his bindings allow. He locks eyes with Tajomaru, shouting over the thunder.*\n\n**Takehiro**: You call it clean? That beast carries not just your loot, but the stench of the rape you committed here and now! It is a cursed burden, rat, and it will drag your shame all the way back to your den!\n\n## Epilogue\n\n*The confrontation reaches a violent stalemate. Masago is physically rebuffed from the horse, tumbling back into the mud, her dagger lost in the scuffle. Tajomaru stands guard over the animal, breathing hard, his escape route effectively poisoned by Takehiro’s words. Takehiro remains bound but triumphant in his verbal condemnation, having turned the bandit's prize into a curse. No one is dead, yet no one can leave—the*",
