   "fullProse": "# In a Grove\n\n## Background\n\nThe bandit Tajomaru has lured the samurai Takehiro and his wife Masago into a remote bamboo grove, where he bound Takehiro to a cedar tree and assaulted Masago in front of him. The act is finished, but the violence has not ceased. Tajomaru’s longsword stands in the mud, Masago clutches a small dagger, and Takehiro remains tied. The three are frozen in the heavy, humid air, each weighing the cost of walking away alive against the weight of their shame.\n\n## Characters\n- **Tajomaru** (bandit): A notorious bandit of the mountain roads, known for both prowess and cunning. — wants: Walk away whole, without looking base — better to end this as a 'duel' than a slaughter.\n- **Masago** (the samurai's wife): Takehiro's wife, robbed while traveling at her husband's side. — wants: To never again be looked at like that; to bring this shame to some end.\n- **Takehiro** (samurai): A samurai traveling with his wife, caught in the trap and bound. — wants: To claw back a shred of a warrior's dignity from this humiliation — even at the price of his own death.\n\n## SCENE 1\n*Deep in a bamboo grove on a shaded slope. Midday light filters through the leaves, casting dappled shadows on the mud. The samurai is bound to a cedar; the bandit stands loose; the wife kneels in the dirt. The bandit's longsword is thrust point-down into the mud between them.*\n\n*Masago crawls forward on her knees, her trembling hand hovering over the hilt of the longsword standing in the mud. She brushes the cold metal, testing its weight, desperate for a weapon that is not her own body.*\n\n*Takehiro twists his wrists against the coarse rope. He cannot reach the longsword, so he jerks his chin sharply at the small dagger tucked in Masago’s obi, his eyes urging her to a different violence.*\n\n*Tajomaru steps forward, placing his boot possessively on the hilt of the longsword, barring her from it. He grins, gesturing toward Masago with a taunting flourish.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: Well, samurai? She offers you a choice. Fight me like a man for her honor, or watch her spill her blood while you sit tied like a hog.\n\n*Masago’s hand moves with sudden, violent clarity to her obi. She draws the short dagger, the metal ringing as it clears the sheath. She turns the point inward, pressing the cold tip against the silk over her heart.*\n\n**Masago**: Do not look at me like that. If this shame is what you see, then I will cut it out of the world right now.\n\n## SCENE 2\n*The grove is stifling, the air thick with the scent of crushed bamboo. Takehiro is bound to the cedar; Masago stands a few paces away with the dagger pressed to her chest; Tajomaru stands near them, shifting his weight in the mud.*\n\n*Takehiro straightens his spine against the rough bark. He ignores the bandit, locking his gaze onto Masago’s face. Slowly, he lets the mask of the terrified husband slip away, replacing it with a slack, unblinking stare of profound shame and disgust.*\n\n**Takehiro**: Do it, then. But know that if you die, it is not as a wronged wife. You leave this world as I see you now.\n\n*Masago’s hand falters, the dagger’s tip trembling as it tears the silk. A tiny bead of blood wells against her pale skin. Her breath hitches, her eyes widening not with fear of death, but with the crushing weight of his silence.*\n\n**Masago**: You... you dare look at me like that?\n\n*Tajomaru clicks his tongue. With a swift motion, he bends down, snatches his longsword from the mud, and slides it into the scabbard at his hip, removing the possibility of a duel from the table.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: A quiet is more terrible than any scream, isn't it? The samurai has spoken louder with his eyes than either of us could with steel.\n\n## SCENE 3\n*The rain pours harder, blurring the bamboo forest into a gray cage. The dagger in Masago's hand is the only sharp point in the world, its tip pressed against the white fabric of her kimono right over her heart. Takehiro stands bound to the cedar, while Tajomaru watches from the mud.*\n\n*Masago drives the tip of the dagger harder against her own chest, denting the skin until a single red bloom appears, soaking into the white silk. She stands rigid as a statue, staring only at Takehiro.*\n\n**Masago**: I will not be looked at with those eyes. Not by you. Not by him. If I am to leave this grove, it will be as a corpse you cannot despise.\n\n*Takehiro takes a heavy, deliberate step forward, the ropes biting into his wrists. He lifts his chin, baring his throat, and locks eyes with his wife, daring her to strike.*\n\n**Takehiro**: Then put it here. If you seek an end to shame, let it be mine. Do not let him walk away while you stain the earth with my failure. Kill me, and be done.\n\n*Tajomaru flicks his fingers sharply toward Masago, then jerks his chin at Takehiro, a silent, mocking accusation that the samurai’s hesitation is what drives the steel into her flesh.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: Look closely, samurai. The blade is keen. One slip of her hand, one wrong word from you, and there is no telling whose blood paints the mud—or whose hand held the hilt when it was done.\n\n## Epilogue\n\n*No one has moved. The samurai stands with his chest bared to the dagger, his eyes locked on his wife in a mixture of challenge and contempt. Masago remains rigid, the point of the dagger piercing the skin over her heart, a bright spot of blood staining her kimono, refusing to lower the blade or strike her husband. Tajomaru watches from the mud, his hand resting on the hilt of his sheathed sword, grinning at the stale*",
