   "endingProse": "*Tajomaru turned his back on the longsword, the dagger, and the samurai, and walked out of the grove toward the horse waiting on the mountain trail. He mounted and rode away, vanishing into the rain. Takehiro remains bound to the cedar, alive but humiliated, and Masago crouches in the mud at his feet, the small dagger lying between them. Neither has moved to kill the other, nor to flee.*",
   "fullProse": "# In a Grove\n\n## Background\n\nHigh in the mountains, the notorious bandit Tajomaru lured the samurai Takehiro and his wife Masago into a secluded bamboo grove with the promise of a buried treasure. It was a trap. Tajomaru bound Takehiro to a cedar tree and assaulted Masago in front of him. The act is finished, but the three remain frozen in the aftermath, trapped in a circle of mud, rain, and unyielding contempt.\n\n## Characters\n- **Tajomaru** (bandit): A notorious bandit of the mountain roads, known for both prowess and cunning. — wants: Walk away whole, without looking base — better to end this as a 'duel' than a slaughter.\n- **Masago** (the samurai's wife): Takehiro's wife, robbed while traveling at her husband's side. — wants: To never again be looked at like that; to bring this shame to some end.\n- **Takehiro** (samurai): A samurai traveling with his wife, caught in the trap and bound. — wants: To claw back a shred of a warrior's dignity from this humiliation — even at the price of his own death.\n\n## SCENE 1\n*A bamboo grove on a shaded slope. Midday light struggles through the leaves. Takehiro is bound tightly to a large cedar. Masago stands nearby, trembling. Tajomaru looms over them. The bandit's longsword stands driven into the soft mud between the men.*\n\n*Masago staggers forward, wrenching the heavy longsword from the mud with both hands. She thrusts the hilt toward Tajomaru, offering it point-first, her arms shaking violently.*\n\n**Masago**: Take it. If you are the warrior you say you are, then take it. Or does a thief need a rope to fight a husband?\n\n*Tajomaru glances at the offered sword, then at the bound samurai. He steps forward, snatching the hilt from her hand, and pulls it free with a sharp ring of steel.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: You want a show? Fine. I’ll cut you down and leave you for the flies, and no one will call it a murder.\n\n*Takehiro leans forward against the biting hemp, straining his shoulders to push his chest toward the point. He looks not at the blade, but into Tajomaru's eyes with heavy judgment.*\n\n**Takehiro**: You are quick with the steel, thief, but slow with the truth. If you kill me here, who watches the road? You will have to run. And what of the mare you tied beyond the trees, and the bags of silk you hid in the brush? A running man cannot lead a laden horse.\n\n## SCENE 2\n*The grove. Rain begins to fall, tapping against the bamboo leaves. The tension has shifted from the standoff to the physical reality of the bindings.*\n\n*Tajomaru moves behind the cedar. With a single, ringing stroke, he slashes through the hemp rope binding Takehiro. He kicks the samurai forward into the mud and retreats, lowering the longsword.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: There. You’re loose. Now pick up your courage, samurai. You wanted a duel? Here is your chance to stand like a man.\n\n*Masago pulls a small dagger from her sleeve. She clutches it until the edge bites into her palm, then extends the hilt toward Takehiro with both hands, the point turned deliberately toward her own breast.*\n\n**Masago**: Take it. If you cannot bear to look at what I have become, then do not look at me any longer. End it.\n\n*Takehiro’s eyes slide over the offered dagger, then up to his wife’s face. His gaze settles there, heavy and unblinking, devoid of tears—only a raw, silent disgust.*\n\n**Takehiro**: Do not touch steel again. It is not for your hands.\n\n## SCENE 3\n*The rain intensifies, turning the earth into a sliding mire. The samurai sits in the mud near the cedar; the bandit stands free; the wife crouches between them.*\n\n*Tajomaru lunges forward, grappling Takehiro and ripping the concealed dagger from the samurai's robes. He flings it clattering onto the stones between them.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: There it lies! It can end your shame, or mine, or hers—but someone must bleed for it today. Pick it up!\n\n*Masago scrambles in the mud, kicking her legs out with a desperate cry. She snatches the severed rope from the ground and casts it far into the dark bamboo, beyond the circle of light.*\n\n**Masago**: There is no need for bindings! The shame is already loose in the world!\n\n*Takehiro taps the flat of the longsword against the root of the cedar—a deliberate, wooden sound. He fixes his eyes on Masago with a stare utterly devoid of warmth.*\n\n**Takehiro**: You see what sits before you, bandit? You speak of duels and valor, but look at her. She is not a prize to be fought over. She is ruin.\n\n*Masago throws herself into the mire, snatching the small dagger and sliding it forcefully across the mud until it clatters against Takehiro’s feet.*\n\n**Masago**: Enough! Pick it up! If you cannot bear the sight of me, then use it. Pierce your own heart, or mine—but do not let the rat decide which of us lives to remember this day!\n\n## SCENE 4\n*Deep in the grove. The rain hammers the bamboo leaves. The longsword rests in the mud, the dagger at the samurai's feet. The horse waits beyond the screen of trees.*\n\n*Masago scrambles up from the mud, pointing a trembling finger through the bamboo screen toward the trail where the horse is tied.*\n\n**Masago**: You think you leave us here in the dirt while you ride away? Every hoofbeat on that mountain road will scream 'thief' to anyone who hears!\n\n*Tajomaru turns his back on the dagger and the longsword. He splashes through the puddles, his stride lengthening as he heads for the darkness of the path.*\n\n**Tajomaru**: Let them scream. I am a bandit, and I live free. You two can rot in your tragedy.\n\n*Takehiro throws his weight against the cedar, the remnants of the rope tearing into his skin as he roars at the retreating back.*\n\n**Takehiro**: You run to the beast you stole! That horse is not your escape, it is the tether that drags you back to hell!\n\n*Tajomaru vanishes into the shadows of the trail, leaving only the sound of his boots fading into the rain.*\n\n## Epilogue\n\n*Tajomaru turned his back on the longsword, the dagger, and the samurai, and walked out of the grove toward the horse waiting on the mountain trail. He mounted and rode away, vanishing into the rain. Takehiro remains bound to the cedar, alive but humiliated, and Masago crouches in the mud at his feet, the small dagger lying between them. Neither has moved to kill the other, nor to flee.*",
